function reorderRows(rows: HTMLElement[], offsets: number[]) {
  rows.forEach((row, i) => {
    const height = row.getBoundingClientRect().height;
    row.style.top = `${offsets[i]}px`;
    row.style.left = `${offsets[i] + height}px`;
  });
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
The loop alternates geometry reads with style writes. Each write to top or left invalidates layout, so the next iteration's getBoundingClientRect cannot be answered from the previous layout and forces a Recalculate Style and Layout pass; with N rows the interaction pays up to N forced layouts, which is exactly what the trace shows as one long task with interleaved recalculation. The fix has two parts. First, batch: read all the geometry in one pass into an array, then perform all the writes in a second pass, so the frame recalculates layout at most once. Second, prefer transforms over top and left: a transform does not trigger layout, the compositor handles the movement, so the writes stop invalidating layout entirely. Rewritten:

```ts
function reorderRows(rows: HTMLElement[], offsets: number[]) {
  const heights = rows.map((row) => row.getBoundingClientRect().height);
  rows.forEach((row, i) => {
    row.style.transform = `translate(${offsets[i]}px, ${offsets[i] + heights[i]}px)`;
  });
}
```

If offsets are stable across interactions, cache the measurements instead of re-reading geometry every time, and reducing the affected DOM further lowers the cost of the one recalculation that remains. After the fix, re-record the same scenario: the trace should show the reads grouped at the start, a single style recalculation (or none, if only transforms are written), and no long task during the interaction. Verify by comparing the trace and the user-facing metric, not by assuming.

code.code:
import { onLCP, onINP } from "web-vitals";

const vitals: Record<string, number> = { lcp: 0, inp: 0, cls: 0 };

onLCP((metric) => { vitals.lcp = metric.value; });
onINP((metric) => { vitals.inp = metric.value; });

new PerformanceObserver((list) => {
  for (const entry of list.getEntries()) {
    const shift = entry as LayoutShift;
    if (!shift.hadRecentInput) vitals.cls += shift.value;
  }
}).observe({ type: "layout-shift" });

window.addEventListener("unload", () => {
  navigator.sendBeacon("/api/vitals", JSON.stringify(vitals));
});

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Problem 1: CLS is hand-rolled as a raw sum of layout-shift values. The metric is not a sum: it aggregates shifts into session windows and reports the largest window score, which is why the dashboard disagrees with CrUX. Use onCLS from the web-vitals library, which implements the metric's defined aggregation rules. Problem 2: reporting only on unload. Unload is not reliable for every lifecycle: mobile users background the page or close tabs without unload firing, which explains the missing samples, and the missing samples skew toward mobile, the population that most needs monitoring. Send when the page becomes hidden (visibilitychange to hidden) as well as on unload. Problem 3: no context. A number without route, release, and device class cannot be segmented or acted on; a regression cannot be located. Problem 4: the layout-shift observer is created without buffered: true, so any shifts that happened before this script ran are never delivered. Rewritten:

```ts
import { onLCP, onINP, onCLS } from "web-vitals";

const RELEASE = "2026-09-17.1"; // injected at build time
const latest: Record<string, number> = {};

for (const report of [onLCP, onINP, onCLS]) {
  report((metric) => { latest[metric.name.toLowerCase()] = metric.value; });
}

function send() {
  navigator.sendBeacon("/api/vitals", JSON.stringify({
    ...latest,
    route: location.pathname,
    release: RELEASE,
  }));
}

document.addEventListener("visibilitychange", () => {
  if (document.visibilityState === "hidden") send();
});
window.addEventListener("unload", send);
```

The library handles buffering and metric aggregation internally, so the design's job is context and reliable delivery. Server-side, report distributions at the 75th percentile per segment (mobile and desktop separately), not averages.

code.code:
type Product = {
  name: string;
  heroUrl: string;
  related: { id: string; name: string; imageUrl: string }[];
};

export function ProductPage({ product }: { product: Product }) {
  return (
    <main>
      <section className="hero" style={{ backgroundImage: `url(${product.heroUrl})` }} />
      <h1>{product.name}</h1>
      <ul>
        {product.related.map((p) => (
          <li key={p.id}>
            <img src={p.imageUrl} alt={p.name} />
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </main>
  );
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Audit: (1) The hero is a CSS background-image, so it is only discovered after the stylesheet is downloaded and parsed; an image declared in the HTML is found during the initial parse. That adds resource-load delay to the likely LCP element. (2) There is a single heroUrl for all devices, so mobile downloads desktop-sized bytes, inflating resource-load duration for the exact segment that regressed. (3) The related-product images have no width or height, so each arrival moves the content below it: a direct CLS source. (4) The related images are eager, so their requests compete for bandwidth with the hero during the window that determines LCP. Refactor:

```tsx
type Product = {
  name: string;
  heroUrl: string;
  heroMobileUrl: string;
  related: { id: string; name: string; imageUrl: string }[];
};

export function ProductPage({ product }: { product: Product }) {
  return (
    <main>
      <picture>
        <source srcSet={product.heroMobileUrl} media="(max-width: 640px)" />
        <img src={product.heroUrl} width={1280} height={720} alt={product.name} />
      </picture>
      <h1>{product.name}</h1>
      <ul>
        {product.related.map((p) => (
          <li key={p.id}>
            <img src={p.imageUrl} width={320} height={240} loading="lazy" alt={p.name} />
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </main>
  );
}
```

The hero is now an img in the HTML (early discovery, eager by default) with responsive sources and intrinsic dimensions; the related images are deferred with loading="lazy" and reserve their space. Then verify: confirm the LCP element in field data, check the waterfall for when the hero request starts, and watch field CLS for the route, because the refactor is only proven when the metrics move.
